---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/product-launch';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/product-launch');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/product-launch');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Product launch campaigns</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Shopify SMS and email: product launch campaigns</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Match access promises to sellable variant inventory. This guide sets out selection criteria and proposed workflows; it does not report hands-on platform tests or merchant campaign results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-8">
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Choose audiences before scheduling</h2>
      <p>A launch is a coordinated release: customers need accurate access times, relevant products and working purchase links. Define VIP, explicit waitlist and general audiences as mutually exclusive send groups. An SMS subscriber is not automatically a VIP, and a waitlist signup must not silently become permission for every marketing channel. Use email to explain the product and SMS selectively for an actual time-sensitive opening.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Supplier fit: Klaviyo campaign audiences</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo documents a campaigns library that creates audience segments and message content for goals including promoting a product. This supports considering it for segmented launch campaigns. It does not prove automatic inventory reservation, protected early access or a zero-delay stock check. Demonstrate those requirements separately in the Shopify storefront and the chosen messaging integration.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Create the launch control sheet</h2>
      <p>Record variant IDs, sellable units, publication time, VIP allocation, public opening time with time zone, audience definitions and a named stop-send owner. Reconcile stock held for other channels. An early-access URL is not an access control: verify the storefront actually enforces the promised restriction. Prepare a sold-out landing state before scheduling any messages.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Three proposed launch designs</h2>
      <p>Streetwear: give a defined VIP group an access window, then send only remaining eligible waitlist customers the public opening. Beauty: collect shade preferences and link to the correct variant without promising reserved stock. Outdoor gear: explain fit and specifications in a teaser, then launch to interested buyers with an honest stock status. These are designs to rehearse, not observed sell-through or conversion results.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Teaser, VIP, public and closing</h2>
      <p>One draft calendar is a teaser a week before, VIP access a day before public opening, and a closing reminder only for a real deadline with stock available. Adapt the gaps to replenishable products versus limited drops. Exclude purchasers from later acquisition messages. Refresh audiences before each wave and decide whether ordinary cart or browse messages should pause during the release.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Handle variant sellout and capacity</h2>
      <p>Avoid embedding a claim that availability is guaranteed when the message can be read hours later. Send customers to a current product page with a clear sold-out or waitlist option. A stock check before dispatch cannot prevent a later sellout. Print-on-demand launches also need production capacity and delivery estimates; unlimited storefront stock does not mean unlimited fulfillment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Partner approvals and post-drop routing</h2>
      <p>Agree creative, logo rights, support ownership and send times with collaboration partners. Each brand must use its own permitted audience; list overlap is not permission to exchange customer records. Route sold-out interest to an explicit back-in-stock signup. Give non-buyers relevant alternatives without immediately discounting the product that early customers bought at full price.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Proposed rehearsal</h2>
      <p>Preview VIP access before and after the gate opens, a sold-out shade, an unsubscribed waitlist member and someone who buys between the first and second wave. Check product publication, link parameters, local send timing and the stop-send procedure. These are proposed merchant tests. No stress test, inventory test or campaign rehearsal has been performed for this page.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Retrospective by wave</h2>
      <p>Record eligible audience size, deliveries, purchases net of returns, contribution after messaging cost, units sold and complaints for each wave. Measure operator setup time if automation is a selection criterion. Fast sell-through alone does not prove SMS was incremental or profitable. Keep a release log of actual send times and stock exceptions to improve the next drop.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Primary supplier reference</h2>
      <p>Checked 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/30892315134747">Klaviyo campaigns library documentation</a>. The supplier fact above is limited to that documented capability. The workflow rules and acceptance exercises are editorial proposals for a merchant to validate.</p>
    </div>
    <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold" aria-label="Related guides">
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">Vip segments →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">Back in stock →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/apps">App directory →</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
